LotR movie trilogy blu-ray.
</a>

<h3>The Acclaimed Film Trilogy</h3>

<p>The <strong>2001 and 2003</strong> cinematic releases prompted a massive new surge of interest in Tolkien's works.</p>

<p>Director <strong>Peter Jackson</strong> helmed this widely acclaimed <em>Lord of the Rings</em> film trilogy, introducing Middle-earth to a global audience.</p>

<h2>Tolkien's Influence on Enya</h2>

<p>A number of rock, classical, and New Age music artists have been deeply influenced by Tolkien's world building.</p>

<p>A long-time fan of the author, Enya wrote an instrumental piece titled <strong>"Lothlórien"</strong> in <strong>1991</strong> for her album <em><a href="/shepherd-moons/">Shepherd Moons</a></em>.</p>

<p>The track pays homage to one of the beautiful <strong>Elvish kingdoms</strong> featured in the novels.</p>

<h3>Joining The Fellowship</h3>

<p>In <strong>2001</strong>, Peter Jackson personally asked Enya to compose music for <em>The Lord of the Rings: The Fellowship of the Ring</em>.</p>

<p>Film composer <strong>Howard Shore</strong> specifically imagined her voice for the score, making a rare exception to feature an outside artist.</p>

<p>Enya travelled to <strong>New Zealand</strong> to watch a rough cut of the film before returning to <strong>Aigle Studio</strong> in Dublin to record.</p>

<h3>The Original Songs</h3>

<p>Working alongside producer <strong>Nicky Ryan</strong> and lyricist <strong>Roma Ryan</strong>, she composed two distinct tracks for the film's soundtrack:</p>

<ul>
<li><a href="/enya-other/#An%EDron"><strong>"Aníron"</strong></a>: Written as the theme for Aragorn and Arwen, featuring lyrics in Tolkien's fictional <strong>Sindarin</strong> language.</li>
<li><a href="/enya-other/#May_It_Be"><strong>"May It Be"</strong></a>: A compositionally simple piece sung in both English and the Elvish tongue of <strong>Quenya</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<p>Shore seamlessly orchestrated these tracks with the <strong>London Philharmonic Orchestra</strong> and <strong>London Voices</strong>.</p>

<h3>Awards and Linguistic Inspiration</h3>

<p>The success of "May It Be" earned Enya prestigious nominations for an <strong>Academy Award</strong> and a <strong>Golden Globe</strong> for Best Original Song.</p>

<p>She famously performed the track live at the <strong>74th Academy Awards</strong> in March <strong>2002</strong>.</p>

<p>The experience of studying Elvish for these songs profoundly impacted lyricist <strong>Roma Ryan</strong>.</p>

<p>It directly inspired her to invent <strong>Loxian</strong>, a brand-new fictional language introduced on Enya's 2005 album, <strong><em>Amarantine</em></strong>.</p>
</Layout>
